        "titulo": "The first use: NEGATION (\"I don't have\")",
        "explicacao": "The first use: NEGATION (\"I don't have\"). Now that you know the endings, here is the first practical use, and it is good news: the negation of possession is the gateway to the genitive. It is the easiest and most frequent use. You already know from lesson 6 how to say \"I have\": У меня́ есть кни́га. (I have a book.) To say \"I don't have a book\", Russian swaps есть for нет and puts the object in the genitive: У меня́ нет кни́ги. (I have no book.) Notice: кни́га became кни́ги. That is the genitive singular. Compare. The logic is simple: Есть (have) + nominative. Нет (don't have) + genitive. Why the genitive? Here is a handy way to picture it: if I have a book, the book is mine, it belongs to me. If I don't have it, nothing belongs to me, and the genitive, the case of belonging, marks that missing belonging. English has a faint hint of this idea in \"I have no book\", where \"no\" works like \"not any\" and you are left with the empty set. Full sentences with \"I don't have\". Notice: all the objects are in the genitive, every one of them. It is a fixed rule: нет always takes the genitive. Careful: some of these sentences have a genitive plural (друзе́й, дете́й, де́нег). That belongs to lesson 28. For now focus on the genitive singular; the singular sentences are the easiest.",
